From 4c2dc851a5d60773cf77a3ad18c3755ae5b19ca6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tengzhen <tengzhen.xiong@tuya.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 18:48:31 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] alientek rk3506: BLE/SoftAP provisioning boot chain

Cut roughly 20-30s off "power on" to "phone can see the device". The old
S39btattach/S41 pair did ~20s of unconditional fixed sleeps on the
success path, and S39's "hciconfig hci0 up" fought bluetoothd, which owns
power-up via mgmt. That made S41's plain bring-up fail every boot and
fall back to its expensive USB unbind/rebind + firmware re-download
recovery round.

- S39btattach: no btattach at all (this board's BT is USB rtk_btusb, not
  UART ttyS5), no hciconfig up, and it no longer blocks rcS - it just
  spawns S41's bring-up in the background so hardware settling overlaps
  the rest of init.
- S41ble_ready: plain mgmt path as round 1, USB rebind demoted to a
  round-2 fallback. Fixed sleeps replaced with 50/100/200ms polling via
  usleep - busybox sleep has no float support, so "sleep 0.05" errors out
  and degenerates into a busy wait.
- S98wukong_ai: blocks on "S41ble_ready wait" for whatever is left of the
  background bring-up (normally returns immediately) instead of starting
  bluetoothd itself.
- S40bluetoothd: defers to S41, which starts bluetoothd only once hci0 is
  healthy; starting earlier means Index Added never arrives.
- S03modules_init: the BLE chain is built into the kernel now, so nothing
  here gates hci0. Remaining modules (wifi, fs, uvc, storage) load in the
  background instead of serializing rcS.
- S01overlayfs: seed /var/lib/dbus and /var/lib/misc after the bind mount
  replaces the squashfs /var/lib, plus the BlueZ STORAGEDIR under
  /userdata. Without this, dbus cannot create machine-id and BlueZ never
  comes up.
- S97soft_rtc: restore wall-clock from /userdata. The board has no
  battery RTC and Tuya cloud TLS fails against a 1970 clock.
- etc/main.conf: LE-only ControllerMode, privacy off so the public BDADDR
  is used in advertising. Rockchip's BlueZ patch sets configdir=/etc, so
  this is the file bluetoothd actually reads.

Add deploy-wukong-app.sh. The app has no buildroot package - it reaches
the rootfs by sitting in fs-overlay - and copying the binary there was
separate from writing the /etc/wukong_ai_build version stamp, so the two
drifted: the stamp claimed 1.0.86 while the shipped binary was 1.0.87.
BLE_NETCFG_NOTES.md tells people to trust that stamp when confirming what
is on the board, so a stale one actively misleads. The script does both
from one source path, or neither.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
